mb|1S|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Although more often than not when I bid 2@S in this situation I have 4 spades. You should not be afraid to raise to 2@S with 3 when you have an appropriate hand. If I had fewer points (for example take away my @CK) and my hand was @SJ108 @HAKxxx @Dx @CAxxx I would definitely bid 2@S. |
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|My nice spades and singleton diamond are the reason why I would do that. But I am going to bid it slightly differently and will explain why in a second.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


mb|2C|mb|P|mb|2H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now back to why I bid 2@C earlier as I am going to bid 2@S next. That shows a medium hand (good 15 or more) with 3 spades, 5 hearts, and usually 1 diamond and 4 clubs. Although it can rarely be 3-5-0-5. So I have now pretty precisely described my hand and North can make an intelligent decision about what to do now. |
PG| ***** 42 ***** |


mb|2S|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I would bid 2@S right away with a weak hand that was 3-5-1-4. When I bid 2@C first and then 2@S that shows more strength. I am talking total points - HCP, length and distribution. I also use Bergen Hand Evaluation which incorporates all those factors.|
PG| ***** 43 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I would pass also.|
PG| ***** 44 ***** |


